# Endings: checked in this order on arrival day; the last one has no conditions.
# Each ending is one carrier clause (the fleet's hail vote) times one arrival clause
# (the storm vote: held the plan, waited it out in orbit, or no storm). Every ending
# closes on the first crate. `title` is the heading; see STORY_BIBLE.md.

card end_lights
type ending
cat ending
title Lights On
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_hold
text The storm took the prime zone overnight, so the fleet goes down at the backup site, into the brown, radar for height and Cargo 2's transponder for place, answered five days out and pinging on time. Then it turns on every floodlight it has: a white smudge right where the terrain map says it should be. Seven ships land three kilometres off. Unloading starts at dawn. The first crate out of the hold says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_through
type ending
cat ending
title Through the Dust
when flag arrive_hold
text Holding the plan means flying the last kilometres blind. Radar gives height; place comes from a light in the brown, every eleven seconds, still asking. Six ships land three kilometres off. The seventh comes down long, on the slope below the rim, and a leg folds under it: crew fine, ship done, it will not fly home. Two sols later the first suit out, the one who argued to answer, climbs the rim and answers from two hundred metres. The recorder stops on HELLO RX · OK. The message to the loading bay at Starbase is short: "Landed. All seven, one for good. Found the eighth."

card end_long_orbit
type ending
cat ending
title The Long Orbit
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted, and the long orbit. Week three, night pass: seven tablets on seven galley tables, ration bars cut in half, and Cargo 2's manifest coming down a line a pass, read aloud in turn. Tonight's line is a spare drill bit, and seven people cheer a drill bit. Below, the storm's edge finally shows ground. A week later seven ships land, and the rover drives out to floodlights that hold steady. In Cargo 2's hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_patience
type ending
cat ending
title Patience
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted. The ground's hold stands, no first handshake without a suit beside it, and from low orbit the crater rim hides it most of each pass. Week two, half portions: the knock comes later every night, and every night it is back. The night before the fleet lands, it isn't. The first suit out, the one who argued to answer, reaches it at dawn: floodlights dark, frost on the wing, the recorder still readable. Two years of REPLAY · DISCARD, and no OK. The answer goes in by hand, from two metres. The message to Starbase: "Landed. All seven. Found the eighth."

card end_friend
type ending
cat ending
title Old Friend
when flag signal_answered
text Seven ships come down in clear air on a wind forecast cross-checked against the weather mast of Cargo 2, thirty kilometres off. Answered five days ago, it has been reporting surface wind ever since, like something that has been rehearsing. On the first drive out, two sols later, it holds its floodlights steady and sends its whole manifest. In Cargo 2's hold, the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_close
type ending
cat ending
title Close Enough
text Seven ships land in clear air. On the first sol Cargo 3's ice drill snaps its only bit, and the fleet's water margin starts going down a day at a time; the spare is on a written-off manifest, thirty kilometres off. On the third sol the rover drives out, the one who argued to answer at the wheel, and answers the next knock from two hundred metres. The screen shows one line: HELLO RX · OK. Then the floodlights go out, and stay out: two years of asking, and the battery had one answer left. The bit is where the manifest said. To Starbase: "Landed. All seven. Found the eighth, in time."
